---
title: Render passes
description: Route post-processing work through source, target, canvas, and named slots.
order: 10
sidebar:
  label: "Render Passes"
---

The `passes` prop accepts render, compute, and fragment-feedback passes. Compute and feedback passes execute before the base material; this page covers the post-scene render-pass graph.

## Slot flow

| Slot | Access | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `source` | Input and output | Current scene or pass result |
| `target` | Input and output | Built-in ping-pong companion |
| `canvas` | Output only | Logical final output |
| Named target | Input and output | Surface declared through `renderTargets` |

A default render pass reads `source`, writes `target`, and sets `needsSwap: true`. After execution, the two logical slots swap, making the output the next `source`.

```ts
const pass = new ShaderPass({
	fragment: `
fn shade(inputColor: vec4f, uv: vec2f) -> vec4f {
	return vec4f(inputColor.rgb * vec3f(uv, 1.0), inputColor.a);
}
`
});
```

Use `needsSwap: false` for explicit routing, including final output:

```ts
const finalPass = new BlitPass({
	needsSwap: false,
	input: 'source',
	output: 'canvas'
});
```

When a color pipeline is active, `canvas` is still the public final slot. Spektral maps it to an internal working surface and presents it to the physical canvas afterward.

## Validation

The planner rejects:

- `needsSwap: true` with anything other than `source → target`;
- `canvas` as an input;
- undeclared named inputs or outputs;
- reads from `target` or a named target before a previous enabled pass writes it in the current frame.

Disabled passes are omitted before validation. Pre-scene passes do not write graph slots and cannot satisfy a render-pass dependency.

## Built-in passes

| Pass | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `ShaderPass` | Samples the input and calls `fn shade(inputColor: vec4f, uv: vec2f) -> vec4f` |
| `BlitPass` | Samples and copies the input with linear or nearest filtering |
| `CopyPass` | Uses `copyTextureToTexture` when valid; otherwise falls back to `BlitPass` |

`CopyPass` can copy directly only when clear is false, preserve is true, source and destination differ, neither is the canvas texture, and dimensions and formats match.

All three share render flags and slot options. [Passes API](/docs/api-passes-reference) is the source of truth for constructors, defaults, and runtime methods.

Spektral prepares built-in fullscreen pipelines asynchronously before their first use. Initial
active passes are prepared together before the first frame, and the render path never creates
shader modules or pipelines. A pass added later, enabled later, or routed to a new target format is
skipped only until its matching pipeline is ready; prepared built-ins and synchronous custom passes
continue to run.

`ShaderPass.setFragment(...)` keeps the last-known-good pipeline visible during compilation. Edits
are latest-request-wins: stale success or failure cannot replace or report over a newer edit. A
current failure is recoverable, while a successful replacement requests another frame.

## Inspecting the graph

`useSpektral().graph.getSnapshot()` exposes the current pre-scene, base-scene, and post-scene plan without rebuilding it. Schema v1 records pass order, slot flow, logical and opaque physical resource IDs, subresources, and every edge reason. The experimental snapshot is cached and deeply frozen. It contains no GPU objects, WGSL, buffer data, callbacks, or timings.

See [Hooks & Context](/docs/hooks-and-context) for identity and lifecycle details and [Hooks API](/docs/api-hooks-reference) for the public types.

## Lifecycle and custom passes

The renderer tracks pass objects by identity. It calls optional `setSize(...)` when a pass first appears and when its effective size changes, and calls optional `dispose()` after removal or renderer teardown.

A custom `RenderPass` implements the synchronous `render(context)` contract and does not need an asynchronous preparation method. The context contains the device, shared command encoder, all public slot surfaces, resolved `input` and `output`, named targets, timing and size, resolved clear/preserve flags, and `beginRenderPass(...)`.

Use `context.output` or `context.beginRenderPass()` rather than addressing the physical swapchain directly. The logical `canvas` surface may be internal when final color presentation is enabled.

Custom passes are supported only through the `RenderPass` contract. Compute and fragment-feedback
passes are managed by Spektral because the renderer owns their resource and texture lifecycle.
Objects with only `isCompute: true` or `isPingPongShader: true` are rejected. Use `ComputePass`,
`PingPongComputePass`, or `PingPongShaderPass` instead.

Explore complete pipelines in the [TanStack playground demo](/playground/tanstack?framework=svelte). See [Render Targets](/docs/render-targets), [Compute Shaders](/docs/compute-shaders), and [Passes API](/docs/api-passes-reference).
